  diseaseId: apple_scab
  diseaseName: Apple Scab
  aiLabelAliases:
  - Apple___Apple_scab
  - Apple___Scab
  - apple_scab
  - scab
  - apple scab
  - Apple Scab Leaf
  pathogenType: 0
  pathogen: 
  affectedPart: 0
  sources: []
  description: A fungal disease (Venturia inaequalis) of apple leaves and fruit.
  causes: Cool, wet spring weather; the fungus overwinters in fallen leaves.
  effectOnPlant: Infected leaves drop early; fruit is blemished and cracks.
  untreatedOutcome: Repeated years of leaf loss weaken the tree and cut cropping.
  symptoms:
  - Olive-green to brown velvety spots
  - Spots merge and leaves yellow
  - Cracked, scabby fruit
  supportingFeatureIds:
  - leaf_spots
  - yellowing
  - leaf_color
  moderateSeverityThreshold: 0.35
  severeSeverityThreshold: 0.7
  treatmentPlan: {fileID: 11400000, guid: eb85615bfc6ea6049abb4ce4087e29d8, type: 2}
  preventionPlan: {fileID: 11400000, guid: 0705f6dc5104509479722abdcfa9a085, type: 2}
  simulationProfile: {fileID: 11400000, guid: c6165157cf24c8b43bbd922b67c6b045, type: 2}
  lifecycleStages:
  - The fungus spends winter in fallen, infected leaves.
  - In spring, rain releases spores that land on young leaves and fruit.
  - Olive-green to brown velvety spots appear and later turn dark and scabby.
  - Infected leaves and fruit drop, and the fungus returns to the leaf litter.
  conditionFactors:
  - Cool, wet spring weather.
  - Leaves staying wet for several hours.
  - Fallen leaves left under the tree.
  - A crowded canopy that dries slowly.
  referencePhotos:
  - {fileID: 2800000, guid: 018fa79584f10ca4c9be4fc34dccb8f5, type: 3}
